# Contributing

Use uv 0.12.21 (compatible policy: >=0.12.21,<0.13), Git, and Python 3.14.8.
uv downloads the development interpreter when necessary. The package supports
Python {{ python_min }} through 3.14; source syntax and typing target its minimum.

## Setup

```bash
git init --initial-branch=main  # only for a newly generated project
uv run --no-project --python 3.14 scripts/bootstrap.py
git add .
git commit -m "Initialize project"
```

Bootstrap installs the contributor environment, generates `uv.lock`, and installs
prek hooks. It never creates commits or remotes. Commit the lockfile for reproducible
development. A fresh clone uses `uv sync --locked --all-groups` followed by
`uv run --locked prek install`. If uv reports an incompatible version, install
the version in `.uv-version` before continuing.

## Checks

```bash
uv run --locked python scripts/check.py
uv run --locked python scripts/check.py --audit
uv run --locked ruff check --fix .
uv run --locked ruff format .
uv run --locked pytest
```

The check command verifies the lockfile, formatting, lint, strict types, and tests.
Commit hooks run only quick Ruff and merge-conflict checks. Select `.venv` as your
editor interpreter and use Ruff and basedpyright extensions with the same installed
versions. Keep source and tests typed; explain any narrow diagnostic suppression.

The full check also builds an sdist and wheel, then runs tests against the installed
wheel outside the checkout. No release is uploaded by these checks.

`--audit` runs pip-audit against the freshly synchronized locked environment,
including contributor tools and excluding the editable project. It needs network
access to advisory services; vulnerabilities and service failures return a failure.
It checks known advisories for the current platform, rather than proving code safe.
For an audit alone after syncing, use `uv run --locked pip-audit --local --skip-editable`.

## Dependencies

Use `uv add` for runtime requirements, `uv add --dev` for contributor tools, and
`uv add --optional FEATURE` for optional features installed by users. Update pins
deliberately, regenerate `uv.lock`, and review the changes. Build-backend changes
must update both `[build-system]` and the build constraints/group.

For a library with runtime dependencies, also test its declared minimum versions
in a separate environment. From a clean, disposable clone, run:

```bash
uv sync --python {{ python_min }} --resolution lowest-direct
uv run --no-sync pytest
```

This intentionally changes the disposable clone's lockfile. It must not change
your main checkout's contributor lockfile. A passing locked development environment
does not establish that older dependency versions work.

## Template updates

Commit your work before an update, then run `uvx copier update` from the checkout.
Review every diff and resolve conflicts before committing. The template preserves
the first-created README; other files participate in Copier's update merge.
Change template answers with `copier update --data`, not by editing
`.copier-answers.yml`. Run bootstrap/checks after accepting an update.

## CI and releases

GitHub Actions runs the full check on Linux for Python {{ python_min }}–3.14 and
on macOS/Windows for 3.14. `UV_PYTHON` selects the matrix interpreter over the local
`.python-version` pin. Coverage XML is saved as a workflow artifact. Dependabot
opens weekly updates for uv dependencies and pinned action commits; review these
and keep `.uv-version`, workflow versions, and uv constraints consistent.
{% if publish_to_pypi %}

Create a GitHub environment named `pypi`, with approval rules if desired. Register
this repository, `release.yml`, and environment `pypi` as a PyPI Trusted Publisher.
Ensure the PyPI distribution name is available. No API token is needed.

Use `uv version` to update the static version and lockfile, commit the change, and
push a matching `v<version>` tag. Release checks require that exact tag, audit the
locked environment, build from the sdist, and test the installed wheel. The publish
job downloads those artifacts and uploads them with OIDC; it does not rebuild.
The workflow runs on version tags only. Test on TestPyPI by registering a separate
publisher and changing the environment URL and uv publish endpoint in that workflow.
{% else %}

PyPI publication is disabled. To enable it, update the Copier answers to select a
license, repository owner, and `publish_to_pypi=true`, then review the generated
release workflow and its Trusted Publisher setup instructions.
{% endif %}
